  "issue_title": "`Certificate.verify()` accepts signatures from certificates that expired after being loaded",
  "issue_body": "<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n    version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n<!--\nTry some of the below if you think they are relevant.\nIt will help us figure out the scope of the bug and how many users it affects.\n-->\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n<!--\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\n\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\n\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\n-->\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- None found.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None found.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n<!-- Include the contents of celery --version below -->\n**Celery version**: `main` branch\n\n<!-- Include the output of celery -A proj report below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n\n##Celery Main Branch\n\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n<!-- Please fill the required dependencies to reproduce this issue -->\n- **Minimal Python Version**: 3.12+\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: `main`\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: 5.6.2\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A (reproduces at the certificate unit level, OS-independent)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n<!-- Please fill the contents of pip freeze below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n\ncelery==5.6.3\ncryptography>=41.0\nkombu==5.6.2\n\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<!--\nPlease provide system dependencies, configuration files\nand other dependency information if applicable\n-->\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A. The issue reproduces at the certificate/serializer unit level and does\nnot require a message broker or result backend.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n<!--\nPlease provide a reproducible test case.\n-->\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom celery.security.certificate import Certificate\nfrom celery.security.key import PrivateKey\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.hashes import SHA256\n\n# Sample cert/key pair from Celery's own test suite\n# (t/unit/security/__init__.py: CERT1 / KEY1)\nCERT1 = \"\"\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\nMIICVzCCAcACCQC72PP7b7H9BTANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQUFADBwMQswCQYDVQQGEwJV\nUzELMAkGA1UECBMCQ0ExCzAJBgNVBAcTAlNGMQ8wDQYDVQQKEwZDZWxlcnkxDzAN\nBgNVBAMTBkNFbGVyeTElMCMGCSqGSIb3DQEJARYWY2VydEBjZWxlcnlwcm9qZWN0\nLm9yZzAeFw0xMzA3MjQxMjExMTRaFw0xNDA3MjQxMjExMTRaMHAxCzAJBgNVBAYT\nAlVTMQswCQYDVQQIEwJDQTELMAkGA1UEBxMCU0YxDzANBgNVBAoTBkNlbGVyeTEP\nMA0GA1UEAxMGQ0VsZXJ5MSUwIwYJKoZIhvcNAQkBFhZjZXJ0QGNlbGVyeXByb2pl\nY3Qub3JnMIGfMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBAQUAA4GNADCBiQKBgQC9Twh0V5q/R1Q8N+Y+\nCNM4lj9AXeZL0gYowoK1ht2ZLCDU9vN5dhV0x3sqaXLjQNeCGd6b2vTbFGdF2E45\n//IWz6/BdPFWaPm0rtYbcxZHqXDZScRpvFDLHhMysdqQWHxXVxpqIXXo4B7bnfnG\nvXhYwYITeEyQylV/rnH53mdV8wIDAQABMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBBQUAA4GBAKA4tD3J\n94tsnQxFxHP7Frt7IvGMH+3wMqOiXFgYxPJX2tyaPvOLJ/7ERE4MkrvZO7IRC0iA\nyKBe0pucdrTgsJoDV8juahuyjXOjvU14+q7Wv7pj7zqddVavzK8STLX4/FMIDnbK\naMGJl7wyj6V2yy6ANSbmy0uQjHikI6DrZEoK\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\"\"\"\n\nKEY1 = \"\"\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIICXQIBAAKBgQC9Twh0V5q/R1Q8N+Y+CNM4lj9AXeZL0gYowoK1ht2ZLCDU9vN5\ndhV0x3sqaXLjQNeCGd6b2vTbFGdF2E45//IWz6/BdPFWaPm0rtYbcxZHqXDZScRp\nvFDLHhMysdqQWHxXVxpqIXXo4B7bnfnGvXhYwYITeEyQylV/rnH53mdV8wIDAQAB\nAoGBAKUJN4elr+S9nHP7D6BZNTsJ0Q6eTd0ftfrmx+jVMG8Oh3jh6ZSkG0R5e6iX\n0W7I4pgrUWRyWDB98yJy1o+90CAN/D80o8SbmW/zfA2WLBteOujMfCEjNrc/Nodf\n6MZ0QQ6PnPH6pp94i3kNmFD8Mlzm+ODrUjPF0dCNf474qeKhAkEA7SXj5cQPyQXM\ns15oGX5eb6VOk96eAPtEC72cLSh6o+VYmXyGroV1A2JPm6IzH87mTqjWXG229hjt\nXVvDbdY2uQJBAMxblWFaWJhhU6Y1euazaBl/OyLYlqNz4LZ0RzCulEoV/gMGYU32\nPbilD5fpFsyhp5oCxnWNEsUFovYMKjKM3AsCQQCIlOcBoP76ZxWzRK8t56MaKBnu\nfiuAIzbYkDbPp12i4Wc61wZ2ozR2Y3u4Bh3tturb6M+04hea+1ZSC5StwM85AkAp\nUPLYpe13kWXaGsHoVqlbTk/kcamzDkCGYufpvcIZYGzkq6uMmZZM+II4klWbtasv\nBhSdu5Hp54PU/wyg/72VAkBy1/oM3/QJ35Vb6TByHBLFR4nOuORoRclmxcoCPva9\nxqkQQn+UgBtOemRXpFCuKaoXonA3nLeB54SWcC6YUOcR\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\"\"\n\ncert = Certificate(CERT1)\nkey = PrivateKey(KEY1)\n\n# Note: this particular bundled sample certificate is itself already\n# expired (valid 2013-07-24 to 2014-07-24), which independently\n# confirms the underlying issue -- Certificate.verify() has never\n# rejected it on expiry grounds despite that. To make the reproduction\n# deterministic and independent of wall-clock time, has_expired() is\n# patched below to make the \"certificate has expired\" state explicit.\n\nsig = key.sign(b\"do-something-dangerous\", SHA256())\n\n# With a certificate believed to be valid, verification succeeds as\n# expected:\nwith patch.object(Certificate, \"has_expired\", return_value=False):\n    cert.verify(b\"do-something-dangerous\", sig, SHA256())\n    print(\"Valid cert: verification succeeded (expected)\")\n\n# Now simulate the certificate having expired since it was loaded\n# (has_expired() == True is exactly what FSCertStore checks once, at\n# load time -- this reproduces the state a long-running worker's\n# CertStore would be in after the certificate's not_valid_after time\n# has passed):\nwith patch.object(Certificate, \"has_expired\", return_value=True):\n    # Expected: Certificate.verify() should raise SecurityError, since\n    # the certificate is expired.\n    #\n    # Actual: verify() does not call has_expired() at all, so the\n    # signature is verified successfully and no exception is raised.\n    cert.verify(b\"do-something-dangerous\", sig, SHA256())\n    print(\"Expired cert: verification still succeeded (BUG)\")\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\n<!-- Describe in detail what you expect to happen -->\n\nA certificate that has expired should no longer be trusted for signature verification, even if it was loaded into the certificate store while it wasstill valid.\n\nIf a certificate is valid when a worker starts and later passes its `not_valid_after` time, subsequent calls to `Certificate.verify()` should reject the certificate rather than continuing to accept signatures made with\nit.\n\nThe existing expiration check in `FSCertStore` should remain in place so that certificates that are already expired are rejected when the certificate store is initialized.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n<!--\nDescribe in detail what actually happened.\nPlease include a backtrace and surround it with triple backticks (```).\n-->\n\n`FSCertStore` checks `cert.has_expired()` when a certificate is loaded into the store. However, `Certificate.verify()` does not perform the same expiration check when the certificate is later used to verify a message.\n\nThis creates a time-of-use gap:\n\n1. A certificate is valid when the worker starts.\n2. `FSCertStore` loads and trusts the certificate.\n3. The certificate remains loaded in memory for the life of the worker\n   process (which, in production, is typically far longer than a\n   certificate's validity window).\n4. Time passes and the certificate reaches its `not_valid_after` time.\n5. `Certificate.verify()` is called for a new signed message.\n6. `verify()` performs cryptographic signature verification without\n   re-checking whether the certificate has expired.\n7. The expired certificate continues to be trusted for as long as the\n   worker process stays up.\n\nThe reproduction above demonstrates this directly: `Certificate.verify()` succeeds identically whether `has_expired()` reports `False` or `True` for the same certificate and signature, proving expiry has no effect on the\nverification outcome.\n\nSeparately, and consistent with this: the sample certificate bundled in Celery's own test suite (`t/unit/security/__init__.py::CERT1`, valid 2013-07-24 to 2014-07-24) is itself long expired, yet is used throughout\n`t/unit/security/test_serialization.py` to successfully sign and verify messages in the existing test suite -- which would not be possible if `Certificate.verify()` enforced expiry.\n\nThe problem is therefore not that expired certificates are accepted when the certificate store is initially created. The problem is that a certificate which was valid when loaded can become expired while the worker is running\nand remain usable for signature verification indefinitely afterward.\n\nA possible fix is to enforce certificate validity at the verification boundary by checking `has_expired()` inside `Certificate.verify()` before performing cryptographic signature verification, while retaining the existing load-time check in `FSCertStore`.\n\n",
